    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "i get that. i'm definitely worried about the Jax and Andre ones being too \"on the nose.\" \n\nbut when you say they're \"designed to represent their backgrounds,\" do you mean that the way i've written their personalities—like being street smart or knowing everyone on the corner—is just naturally how those characters are expected to act in a realistic setting? like, is it a trope if it's just a reflection of their environment, or is there a specific reason why those traits feel \"stereotypical\" rather than just grounded?\n\nalso, why do you think Kenji doesn't quite fit either of the two genres as easily as the others?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
